WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.750
INTRO:
Have you ever heard of a site called mega.nz?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.420
On the surface, the site looks like a strong
competitor to Google Drive.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.260
They offer 20 gigabytes of storage, great
encryption, and phenomenal download and upload

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.280
speeds.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.771
Considering this, you would think that Mega
is a great alternative for Google Drive or

00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:18.320
iCloud, but the site is rarely used for the
same cases.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.560
People using mega aren’t saving personal
pictures, videos, or documents.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.279
Rather, they’re uploading and sharing the
latest movies, games, and software.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.200
And instead of the encryption providing safety
for its users, it actually makes it harder

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.270
for copyright owners to track down what’s
being shared.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.360
Any other founder in this situation would
probably freak out given that they don’t

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.329
want to be hunted down by the FBI.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:44.450
But, the founder of Mega, Kim Dotcom, isn’t
phased by the FBI.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.989
In fact, the FBI was trying to hunt him down
when he launched Mega, and he’s still one

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:51.239
of the most wanted people in the world.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:56.030
So, here’s the wild story of Kim Dotcom
and how Mega became the go-to cloud platform

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.910
for pirates.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:03.340
EARLY TROUBLE:
Taking a look back, Kim never had a particularly

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.340
calm life.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:10.280
He was born on January 21, 1974, in West Germany,
and from a very young age, he was interested

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.320
in hacking.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.229
And when I say hacking, I don’t mean hacking
league of legends or world of warcraft.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:22.350
No, Kim over here was hacking PBX systems
or private branch exchange systems of US companies.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.510
When he was just 18 years old, he was hacking
the likes of NASA, Citibank, and the Pentagon,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.340
and he was selling their access codes for
$200.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.939
If you ask me, I think Kim was the one that
was really being robbed here.

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:38.740
Anyway, in the beginning, Kim wanted to become
a cybersecurity consultant, but these dreams

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.390
would be crushed very early on.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.110
You see, prosecutors weren’t exactly happy
that this 18-year-old was hacking into national

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.860
systems and selling access to protected data.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.409
So, they kept an extremely close eye on Kim,
and it didn’t take them long to arrest him.

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:56.780
In March of 1994, German police arrested Kim
for selling stolen phone numbers and they

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.540
threw him in jail for a month.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.899
Right when he got out, they pressed hacking
charges on Kim, and Kim ended up being convicted

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:06.860
for 11 counts of computer fraud and 10 counts
of data espionage.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.840
This itself could’ve gotten him locked up
for years, but since many of the crimes were

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.540
committed when he was a minor, the judge decided
to go easy on him.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.480
The judge simply gave him a two-year suspended
sentence and wrote his crimes off as “youthful

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.480
foolishness”.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.430
While Kim didn’t have to spend too much
time in prison, given his criminal record,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.030
it was going to be nearly impossible to get
a job.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.080
But, Kim didn’t let this slow him down.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.040
In fact, he used his arrest and conviction
to boost his notoriety and fame within the

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.450
hacking space.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.410
Kim became known as the child prodigy hacker,
and Kim leveraged this to make a living.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.440
He launched a cybersecurity company called
Data Protect.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.850
It’s not clear when exactly he launched
the company, but there are emails suggesting

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.550
that the company was operating as early as
1994.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.720
Anyway, one of their first big contracts was
with Lufthansa airlines.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.690
The story goes that Kim secured a contract
with Lufthansa after showing them how easily

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.849
he could hack into the company’s systems.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:01.750
But others argue that this is not actually
what went down.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.269
First of all, they say it wasn’t Kim that
did the hacking, it was actually one of his

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:07.269
accomplices.

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:11.060
And secondly, the main reason Lufthansa gave
him the contract was not because they were

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.610
impressed by the hacking but because Kim had
insider contacts.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:18.850
We’ll probably never find out what truly
went down, but it doesn’t really matter

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:22.120
for Kim as he started raking in millions from
such contracts.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.390
With this new influx of cash, Kim went on
a spending spree purchasing supercars, houses,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:27.769
and helicopters.

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:31.831
This is when Kim’s public persona shifted
from being a nerd hacker to being a rich hacker

00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:32.831
genius.

00:03:32.831 --> 00:03:35.990
And let’s just say, Kim loved to play into
this persona.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:41.430
LIVING THE FANTASY:
Aside from buying expensive toys, Kim spent

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.019
significant amounts of money on convincing
people that he was the hacker of the people.

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:49.760
One example of this was a short animation
film that he produced called Kimble Special

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.760
Agent.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.030
The premise of the film is Kim breaking into
Microsoft’s headquarters to confront Bill

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.030
Gates.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:59.319
When Kim finally gets to Bill after a crazy
sequence of cars, boats, and helicopters,

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:02.189
it’s exposed that Bill is actually using
an Apple MacBook.

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:06.849
And, Kim goes ahead and carves out the word
Linux on Bill’s wall while Bill pees his

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:07.849
pants.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:12.130
It’s quite an odd story, but I gotta say
it is amusingly entertaining.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.780
Shortly after, Kim also produced another short
film called Kimble Goes Monaco.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:20.859
In this film, Kim was just minding his own
business and trying to enjoy his vacation.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:24.680
But for some reason, Bill Gates was spying
on him the entire time.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.509
When the film launched, it was mostly taken
as a satirical jab at Bill, but with time,

00:04:29.509 --> 00:04:33.220
more and more people have started to believe
that it’s actually true.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.889
Aside from exposing Bill Gates, Kim spent
his time building an internet-based luxury

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:38.710
car called the Megacar.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.080
The vision was for the car to have a computer,
a router, a video conferencing system, and

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.780
a 17-inch display.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.470
The car was supposed to launch for $90,000,
but the launch date never came around.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.900
Similar to the car being a false promise,
many critics argue that Kim’s entire persona

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.740
was a false promise.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.570
According to a New Zealand magazine, Kim was
once spotted taking pictures at an airport

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:01.840
inside parked airplanes and acting as if they
were his own.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.669
While Kim may not have been as rich as he
showed off to be, Kim was doing better than

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:06.669
ever before.

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:12.080
In 2000, he sold 80% of Data Protect to a
German conglomerate named TUV Rheinland.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.280
The acquisition price was not revealed but
it was likely in the tens of millions.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.770
Kim could’ve called it quits right here
and lived a lavish life for the rest of his

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:20.770
days.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:24.350
But Kim was never attracted to the dark side
because of the money, he liked the dark side

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.270
because of the thrill, and it didn’t take
him very long to push it too far.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.930
FLEEING TO SAFETY:
In January of 2001, when the Dot-Com crash

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.400
was starting to take full effect, a company
called LetsBuyIt.com was nearing bankruptcy.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.410
Instead of letting the company die though,
Kim went ahead and purchased 375,000 euros

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:47.060
worth of shares and announced that he was
going to help the company raise 50 million

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.060
euros to get through the year.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.880
Once this news came out, LetsBuyIt stock exploded
and Kim made 1.5 million euros on his initial

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.620
investment at which point he sold his stake.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.350
And as you would guess, the 50 million euro
investment never came.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:03.569
This was clearly a pump and dump and prosecutors
were once again on the hunt for Kim.

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:07.830
But this time, it wasn’t just the prosecutors
that were after Kim either.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.750
After seeing his dubious investment strategy,
the hacking community also turned on Kim.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:16.290
In an effort to save face, Kim claimed that
he had hacked into Osama’s Sudanese bank

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.819
account and he put a $10 million bounty on
Osama’s location.

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:23.810
Kim also launched a group called Young Intelligent
Hackers Against Terrorism or YIHAT.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.730
But, this publicity stunt didn’t work.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:31.040
One hacker named Fluffy bunny actually hacked
YIHAT’s website and defaced the website.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.110
And with that, Kim knew that he was in trouble
and he fled to Thailand.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.509
Unfortunately for Kim, Thailand ended up arresting
him and deporting him back to Germany.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:43.760
Kim pleaded guilty to the charges, and somehow,
he was released with a slap on the wrist once

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.760
again.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.810
He was sentenced to just 20 months probation
and a 100,000 euro fine.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.550
Again, you would think that Kim would just
lie low and play it safe.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.440
But instead, he decided to start a hedge fund.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.990
In December of 2002, Kim moved to Hong Kong
and founded a new business called Kimpire

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:00.990
Limited.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:04.389
The company’s first subsidiary was called
Trendax which promised to leverage artificial

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:06.840
intelligence to generate 25% annual returns.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:11.400
Given Kim’s past though, he was not actually
allowed to take in investments or trade.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:16.090
Nonetheless, Kim tried to lure in investors
using the crazy returns, but this didn’t

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.210
lead him anywhere.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:21.630
So, Kim tried launching more businesses including
Data Protect Limited, Investor Limited, and

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.669
Monkey Limited.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:26.919
None of these really caught steam though until
he delved into the file-sharing business.

00:07:26.919 --> 00:07:31.110
MEGA UPLOAD:
By the time Kim launched the file-sharing

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.820
business though, he was well aware that his
reputation was toast.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.300
So, he decided to start from scratch and rebuild
a new reputation.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:42.470
He started off by legally changing his name
from Kim Schmitz to Kim Dotcom.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:46.169
He also used holding companies to distance
himself from the final product, and he changed

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:49.270
the name of Data Protect Limited to Megaupload.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.790
Megaupload was quite early for its time given
that it was launched in 2005, so Kim had a

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.750
pretty good shot at building a reputable file-sharing
system.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:00.139
But knowing Kim, I don’t think you’d be
surprised to hear that a large portion of

00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:04.759
his customers were using the service to share
movies, music, and cracked software.

00:08:04.759 --> 00:08:08.759
Megaupload offered faster download speeds
for a fee and many users happily paid the

00:08:08.759 --> 00:08:09.759
price.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:13.090
This was extremely lucrative for Kim, and
he was finally making the type of money that

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.289
he had been claiming to make for years.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:20.509
Kim followed up Megaupload with a dozen services
including Megavideo, megapix, megalive, megapay,

00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:21.540
and so on and so forth.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:26.000
In the meantime, the FBI thought that the
entire thing was a Mega Fraud and started

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.470
to hunt down Kim once again.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.289
Kim, however, was no longer in Hong Kon.

00:08:30.289 --> 00:08:33.800
He had since moved to New Zealand and acquired
permanent residency.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.930
But wait a minute, how did a person with criminal
conviction secure residency so quickly?

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:40.370
Well, Kim basically bought his way in.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:44.430
He invested $24 million into a mansion in
Auckland and he put $10 million in government

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:45.430
bonds.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:50.020
These investments made Kim an “investor
plus” in New Zealand which granted him residency.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.930
The FBI wasn’t going to let this move stop
them though.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.920
Every second that they wasted was millions
of dollars in losses for copyright holders.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:01.040
At the peak of MegaUpload, the site boasted
$175 million in revenue, 50 million daily

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.730
visitors, and accounted for 4% of all internet
traffic.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.790
It took the FBI some time, but on January
5, 2012, 3 indictments were filed against

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:14.060
Kim which included racketeering, conspiracy
to commit copyright infringement, and conspiracy

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.269
to commit money laundering.

00:09:15.269 --> 00:09:19.700
And just two weeks later, the New Zealand
police raided his house with 76 officers and

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.110
two helicopters.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:25.380
You might think that this was a bit overkill,
and that’s because it was.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.560
New Zealand literally sent their anti-terrorism
special tactics group to his mansion.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.649
They ended up seizing 18 luxury cars, several
large TVs, and $175 million in cash.

00:09:34.649 --> 00:09:39.500
Shortly after they also froze his 64 bank
accounts and his numerous PayPal accounts.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:43.820
And to top it all of, they threw Kim into
Mt Eden prison and shut down Megaupload.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.750
It seemed like this is where Kim’s story
would come to an end, but really, the FBI

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.610
hadn’t accomplished anything.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:53.370
All they had done was poke the bear.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.370
MEGA.NZ
Over the next few years, Kim’s lawyers and

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:01.640
prosecutors fought back and forth about extradition,
jail time, and fines.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:05.640
You would think that this was an easy case
for prosecutors, but that wasn’t really

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:06.640
the case.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.860
You see, prosecutors had pulled a lot of strings
to arrest Kim.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:14.160
First of all, the search warrant was too broad,
and New Zealand’s court of appeals found

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.810
that the FBI’s taking of information was
illegal.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:20.570
Secondly, the New Zealand police had been
spying on Kim for weeks before they raided

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:25.510
his house, and in New Zealand, it is illegal
to spy on permanent residents and citizens.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:29.760
And finally, they had used excessive force
when raiding his house as they literally sent

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.209
an anti-terrorist squad against him.

00:10:32.209 --> 00:10:35.650
Kim was able to use all these infractions
plus a few others to come to a confidential

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.980
settlement with the police in November of
2017.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:44.350
So, the FBI wasn’t really able to do anything
about Kim, but at least they shut down MegaUpload

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:45.350
right?

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.980
Well, they did indeed shut down Megaupload,
but Kim went ahead and launched a new file

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:53.930
sharing and cloud storage website called Mega.nz
exactly one year after the raid on January

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:55.230
19, 2013.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.329
Kim doesn’t want any more trouble with the
authorities though, so he’s made it clear

00:10:59.329 --> 00:11:02.269
that Mega is not for piracy.

00:11:02.269 --> 00:11:06.890
Kim wants everyone to know that he’s not
a piracy king, and he does not support piracy

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.270
by any means.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:12.690
Mega is designed to be a competitor to Google
Drive, and its high level of encryption is

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.180
simply designed to protect sensitive personal
data.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:16.180
That’s it.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:20.430
Similarly, the insanely fast upload and download
speeds are just a perk for users.

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:23.560
It’s not for downloading massive games and
software.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:27.760
In fact, if you’re a copyright owner and
find any of your content on Mega, you can

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.269
contact Mega, and Kim is more than happy to
take down the content.

00:11:31.269 --> 00:11:36.290
If we’re being real though, mega is just
a relaunch of MegaUpload, but this time, Kim

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:39.920
is being careful to stay on the legal side
of things as much as possible.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.260
And that’s how Kim Dotcom became the internet’s
most wanted man.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.940
Have you guys ever used Mega and what are
your thoughts on piracy?

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:47.860
Comment that down below.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:51.091
Also, drop a like if you don’t support piracy
either just like Kim.

00:11:51.091 --> 00:11:55.490
And of course, consider joining our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:05.550
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

